pra: put the panic backtrace on the screen, wait out the flush lock

The 0158b boot finally reached the target panic, and the capture still
lost: "PRA BT:" frames never appeared, ring15 has no backtrace, and the
flush printed "lock stuck, skipping". Two independent causes, both
fixed here.

1. The backtrace only went into the RAM ring, and the ring only
   survives if pra_log_panic_flush writes it to eMMC. That flush gave
   up after ~1 ms (100 tries x 10000 spins) while the controller
   stalls for seconds - the stats recorded 13.3 s in one write - so it
   skipped exactly under the IO load where panics happen. The panic
   text itself proves console output works on this path, so print the
   frames with printf: pra_log_panic_backtrace now writes "PRA BT:"
   lines to the screen, where a photo captures them regardless of the
   eMMC. Direct pra_log_append drops away entirely.

2. 0158 appended every _cnputs buffer to the ring *before* the
   disableConsoleOutput check, while the per-character loop below
   already appends via _cnputc - ring15 shows every console line
   doubled and interleaved. The append now lives inside the
   disableConsoleOutput branch: live console output reaches the ring
   exactly once through _cnputc, replay text printed while the console
   is disabled (the framebuffer-claim window) reaches it exactly once
   through this branch. That is what makes the printf backtrace safe:
   it hits the ring once either way.

3. pra_log_panic_flush now waits up to 5 s, one delay(1000) per
   try_lock, instead of ~1 ms of spinning - delay() is the busy-wait
   interval from kern/clock.c, safe on the panic path. If the lock is
   still held after 5 s it is stolen: zero the 64-bit state word
   (opaque[1]; the bsd lck_mtx_t is opaque[2] and the arm64 MACH view
   is tsid:type, grp, state, so index 1 is the state in both layouts),
   which clears owner/ilocked/waiters while keeping the group pointer
   valid, then try_lock again. A parked holder will never release, and
   a live flusher has had far longer than a tick; the kernel halts
   after this flush, so a racy disk copy is an acceptable price for
   not skipping.
diff --git a/bsd/dev/pra_sdhci.c b/bsd/dev/pra_sdhci.c
--- a/bsd/dev/pra_sdhci.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/pra_sdhci.c
@@ -922,13 +922,16 @@ pra_log_pick_explicit_lba(void)
 }
 
 /*
- * Dump the frame-pointer chain into the persistent ring. The 0156 panic
- * died a second time before the diagnostics pass printed any backtrace,
- * so the panic caller was unrecoverable. Called from zone_require_panic
- * before panic() runs. Direct pra_log_append, not printf: the console
- * can be inside its disableConsoleOutput window and the msgbuf replay
- * bypasses the ring hook entirely. Frames are validated for alignment
- * and monotonic growth so a corrupt chain cannot loop.
+ * Dump the frame-pointer chain. The 0156 panic died a second time before
+ * the diagnostics pass printed any backtrace, so the panic caller was
+ * unrecoverable. Called from zone_require_panic before panic() runs.
+ * printf, not direct pra_log_append: the panic text on screen proves the
+ * console works on this path, and since 0159 _cnputs only appends when
+ * disableConsoleOutput short-circuits the per-character loop, printf
+ * reaches the ring exactly once via _cnputc (or the disabled branch) and
+ * the screen - the screen copy is the capture that survives even when
+ * the eMMC flush loses. Frames are validated for alignment and monotonic
+ * growth so a corrupt chain cannot loop.
  */
 void
 pra_log_panic_backtrace(void *fp_in)
@@ -950,7 +953,7 @@ pra_log_panic_backtrace(void *fp_in)
 		if (n > (int)sizeof(buf)) {
 			n = (int)sizeof(buf);
 		}
-		pra_log_append(buf, n);
+		printf("%s", buf);
 		fp = next;
 	}
 }
@@ -958,10 +961,10 @@ pra_log_panic_backtrace(void *fp_in)
 /* The flusher thread dies with the boot, so a panic loses everything printed
  * since its last 10 s tick - the pe_init.c:314 panic (0153) left no trace in
  * the ring for exactly this reason. Called once from the panic path, after
- * the paniclog is printed and with the other CPUs parked. Best effort: if the
- * eMMC lock is held (the flusher was interrupted mid-write) skip; rewriting
- * the whole used ring instead of tracking the flusher's local offset keeps
- * this independent of wherever that thread got to. */
+ * the paniclog is printed. Rewriting the whole used ring instead of tracking
+ * the flusher's local offset keeps this independent of wherever that thread
+ * got to. The eMMC lock is waited for, then stolen (see below); a racy disk
+ * copy is acceptable, the screen backtrace is the guaranteed capture. */
 void
 pra_log_panic_flush(void)
 {
@@ -974,19 +977,37 @@ pra_log_panic_flush(void)
 	}
 	/*
 	 * A single try used to skip silently when the periodic flusher owned
-	 * the lock mid-write; the eMMC stalls for seconds at a time, and
-	 * this is the one flush that must not be skipped. Bound the wait
-	 * and complain if the lock is truly stuck (a parked CPU holding it
-	 * will never release).
+	 * the lock mid-write; the eMMC stalls for seconds at a time and this
+	 * is the one flush that must not be skipped. 0158's ~1 ms spin loop
+	 * (100 x 10000) lost exactly the boot it was built for, so wait up
+	 * to 5 s, one delay(1000) per try; delay() is the busy-wait interval
+	 * from kern/clock.c and is safe on the panic path.
+	 *
+	 * If the lock is still held after that, steal it: a parked CPU that
+	 * held the lock will never release, and a flusher stalled mid-write
+	 * on a live CPU has had 5 s - far longer than a normal tick. The bsd
+	 * view of lck_mtx_t is opaque ({uintptr_t opaque[2]}); the MACH view
+	 * is tsid:type, grp, then the 64-bit state word, so opaque[1] is the
+	 * state in either layout. Zeroing just that word clears owner,
+	 * ilocked and the wait queues while keeping the group pointer valid
+	 * for try_lock/unlock. We are panicking: a holder that ever wakes
+	 * will find the mutex moved under it, but the kernel halts after the
+	 * flush and the printf backtrace above is the capture that counts.
 	 */
-	unsigned tries = 0;
+	unsigned ms = 0;
 	while (!lck_mtx_try_lock(&pra_emmc_lock)) {
-		if (++tries > 100u) {
-			printf("PRA LOG: panic flush: lock stuck, skipping\n");
-			return;
-		}
-		for (volatile unsigned spin = 0; spin < 10000u; spin++) {
+		if (ms >= 5000u) {
+			printf("PRA LOG: panic flush: lock stuck, stealing\n");
+			((volatile uintptr_t *)(void *)&pra_emmc_lock)[1] = 0;
+			if (!lck_mtx_try_lock(&pra_emmc_lock)) {
+				printf("PRA LOG: panic flush: steal failed, "
+				    "skipping\n");
+				return;
+			}
+			break;
 		}
+		ms++;
+		delay(1000);
 	}
 	total = os_atomic_load(&pra_log_total, relaxed);
 	used = (total + 511u) & ~511ull;
diff --git a/osfmk/console/serial_console.c b/osfmk/console/serial_console.c
--- a/osfmk/console/serial_console.c
+++ b/osfmk/console/serial_console.c
@@ -321,19 +321,19 @@ _cnputs(char *c, int size, bool poll)
 	a5xelte_log_append(c, size);
 #endif
 
+	if (disableConsoleOutput) {
 #if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_PRA)
-	/*
-	 * Output printed while disableConsoleOutput is set - the framebuffer
-	 * claim window - never reaches _cnputc, and the msgbuf replay that
-	 * redraws it goes straight to vcputc, so the persistent ring would
-	 * lose exactly the text around a panic. Append before the early
-	 * return; when the console is live the per-character hook in _cnputc
-	 * keeps counting, so nothing is doubled.
-	 */
-	pra_log_append(c, size);
+		/*
+		 * Output printed while disableConsoleOutput is set - the
+		 * framebuffer claim window - never reaches _cnputc, and the
+		 * msgbuf replay that redraws it goes straight to vcputc, so
+		 * the persistent ring would lose exactly the text around a
+		 * panic. Append inside this branch, which returns before the
+		 * per-character loop: 0158 put it before the check and every
+		 * live console byte landed twice (once here, once in _cnputc).
+		 */
+		pra_log_append(c, size);
 #endif
-
-	if (disableConsoleOutput) {
 		return;
 	}
 
